+# Keep sessions in memory
+
+For whoever changes login or deploys the web app. Sessions live in the web process's memory because volunteers
+requested removing the Postgres session query from every request to make the app faster.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres was chosen so deploys would preserve sessions without
+adding a service. Volunteers have now requested a process-local dictionary to remove the session database query
+from each request. This changes the earlier priority of preserving logins across deploys; the previous multi-server
+and restart limitations return.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: can keep requests on one server, but cannot preserve sessions across a
+  restart.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; lost on the second force, a service the volunteers would run only
+  for sessions.
+- **Postgres**: previously chosen for shared, persistent sessions; replaced at the volunteers' request to remove
+  the query on every request.
+- **Process-local dictionary**: chosen to remove session database access without adding a service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Use a dictionary in the web app's process, exposed by `sessions` in `larder/session_store.py`, to remove the
+Postgres session query. No speedup has been measured.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session reads and writes no longer require Postgres. Sessions disappear on restart or deploy and are not shared
+between processes. The [session limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits) describe the operating workarounds.
